{"step_index":308,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-25T18:50:58Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nthis caht is done in google ai mode and which is realy help that we can do iwthout authorisation or api key tell me :;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;AI Mode conversation: nexlev antigravity mcp\nYou said: nexlev antigravity mcp\nnexlev antigravity mcp\nYou can connect the [NexLev](https://www.nexlev.io/) Model Context Protocol (MCP) server to Google Antigravity to let AI agents query YouTube niche and market data directly inside your development environment. [[1](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TwRPGmBKIY0&vl=en), [2](https://www.nexlev.io/), [3](https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=Google.google-antigravity)]\nWhat is NexLev MCP?\nData Access: It connects your AI assistant to YouTube niche research data.\nCost: It is free for active NexLev account holders.\nSetup: It links quickly without requiring a separate credit card. [[1](https://www.nexlev.io/)]\nHow to Configure in Antigravity\nOpen the Agent Panel or view in your Google Antigravity workspace.\nAccess the menu options to open MCP Servers or the MCP Store.\nAdd your server settings or configuration via the global or workspace setup file (such as mcp_config.json). [[1](https://medium.com/google-developer-experts/google-antigravity-mcp-servers-how-to-connect-github-and-push-code-with-just-a-prompt-part-a7ca16645403), [2](https://antigravity.google/docs/cli/mcp/), [3](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TwRPGmBKIY0&vl=en), [4](https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=Google.google-antigravity)]\nFor a visual guide on how to add MCP servers to the platform:\n12:11\nHow to Add MCP Servers to Google's Antigravity IDE\n50K views · 8 months ago\nYouTube · Snyk\nWould you like help writing the JSON configuration code or connecting your specific NexLev account credentials?\nYouTube·Snyk\nHow to Add MCP Servers to Google's Antigravity IDE\nrecently Google announced the Gemini 3 Pro and alongside it their new agentic. development platform called anti-gravity google say...\n12:11\nNexLev\nNexLev - AI-Powered YouTube Niche Finder\nYes, Nexlev MCP is available to all Nexlev account holders at no extra cost. You can connect in under 30 seconds — no credit card ...\nVisual Studio Marketplace\nGoogle Antigravity\nAntigravity for Visual Studio Code Bring Google's agent-first development platform to Visual Studio Code. Antigravity pairs Google...\nShow all\ni donot have api keys and it donet reuqired in mcp of nexlev it works fine in cluase but for antigreaivy how can i do nexleve has not given any thing or if it is present and people do buypass this tell me form readl users expericne\nNexLev uses browser-based OAuth authentication rather than a static API key, which is why NexLev does not give you an API key for its MCP server. [[1](https://www.nexlev.io/mcp)]\nIn Claude.ai or ChatGPT, this works seamlessly because those web apps handle the interactive browser login popup automatically. However, desktop/terminal environments like Google Antigravity require specific configuration to connect remote streamable HTTP / OAuth MCP servers. [[1](https://www.nexlev.io/mcp), [3](https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/antigravity-agent)]\nMethod 1: Connect via Remote MCP URL (Standard Antigravity Setup)\nGoogle Antigravity supports remote Streamable HTTP / SSE MCP endpoints. You can add NexLev's endpoint directly to your Antigravity configuration: [[1](https://www.nexlev.io/mcp), [2](https://antigravity.google/docs/mcp/), [3](https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/antigravity-agent)]\nOpen your Antigravity workspace.\nLocate the MCP configuration file:GUI / Settings: Open the Agent panel → click the three dots (...) → MCP Servers → View raw config.\nFile Path: ~/.gemini/antigravity/mcp_config.json (or .agents/mcp_config.json in your project root). [[1](https://antigravity.google/docs/mcp/), [2](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aQDBq-cOglE), [3](https://github.com/czlonkowski/n8n-mcp/blob/main/docs/ANTIGRAVITY_SETUP.md), [4](https://developers.google.com/workspace/guides/universal-search-mcp)]\nAdd the NexLev remote MCP server entry:json\n{ \"mcpServers\": { \"nexlev\": { \"serverUrl\": \"https://prod.dashboard.nexlev.io/api/claude-mcp\" } } }\nUse code with caution.\nSave the file and restart/refresh your MCP servers. [[1](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TwRPGmBKIY0), [2](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aQDBq-cOglE)]\nTrigger a prompt in Antigravity asking to research a YouTube niche. If prompted by the agent with an auth link or OAuth callback, authorize it through your browser window. [[1](https://www.nexlev.io/mcp), [2](https://developers.google.com/workspace/guides/universal-search-mcp)]\nMethod 2: Common Workarounds if Antigravity OAuth Fails\nBecause remote OAuth endpoints in desktop developer environments can sometimes fail to complete the browser redirect, users rely on two primary workarounds:\nThe Stdio / Node Proxy Bridge (Local Relay):If Antigravity cannot complete the cloud OAuth handshake directly, you can run a local proxy script using npx or mcp-remote to bridge your authenticated session from localhost to Antigravity over stdio. [[1](https://github.com/czlonkowski/n8n-mcp/blob/main/docs/ANTIGRAVITY_SETUP.md), [2](https://github.com/upstash/context7/issues/1043)]\nSession Cookie / Bearer Token Header Injection:Log in to [NexLev Dashboard](https://dashboard.nexlev.io/), open your browser's Developer Tools (F12 → Application / Storage → Cookies or Network tab), copy your active session bearer token or cookie, and supply it via headers in mcp_config.json:json\n{ \"mcpServers\": { \"nexlev\": { \"serverUrl\": \"https://prod.dashboard.nexlev.io/api/claude-mcp\", \"headers\": { \"Authorization\": \"Bearer YOUR_COPIED_SESSION_TOKEN\" } } } }\nUse code with caution.\n[[1](https://www.nexlev.io/mcp), [2](https://antigravity.google/docs/mcp/)]\nIf you run into an authentication error or timeout when executing a query in Antigravity, share the exact error output so we can configure the appropriate bridge or header setup.\nNexLev\nNexLev MCP — YouTube AI Research Toolkit with 60+ Tools\nSign up free at dashboard.nexlev.io. 2. Go to claude.ai → Settings → Connectors → Add custom connector. 3. Enter these details: Na...\nGoogle Antigravity\nMCP | Google Antigravity Docs\nOn this page * Antigravity SDK Section titled “Antigravity SDK” In Python applications built using the Antigravity SDK, MCP server...\nGoogle AI for Developers\nAntigravity agent | Gemini API | Google AI for Developers\n18 Aug 2026 — You can connect the Antigravity agent to external tools by registering remote Model Context Protocol (MCP) servers. The agent supp...\nShow all\ngo deeper and find redit user and github user waht they do for this problem how they connect nexlev mcp to antigravity\nReal-world users on GitHub and Reddit face this exact problem when dealing with \"Cloud-Hosted / SSE\" MCP servers that lack conventional API keys and rely on web-app-only integrations. [[1](https://www.reddit.com/r/mcp/comments/1mzm66w/how_is_everyone_using_mcp_right_now/), [2](https://dashboard.nexlev.io/docs/mcp/quickstart)]\nBecause Google Antigravity is a desktop IDE workspace running on your machine, it natively looks for local stdio executable files (like Node scripts or Python binaries) and doesn't inherently know how to pop open a standard web browser redirect to complete an OAuth loop like Claude.ai or ChatGPT. [[1](https://dashboard.nexlev.io/docs/mcp/quickstart), [2](https://github.com/czlonkowski/n8n-mcp/blob/main/docs/ANTIGRAVITY_SETUP.md)]\nHere is how experienced users bypass this restriction to get NexLev working in desktop environments:\n🔎 The Real-User Fix: Injecting the Cookie Header\nSince NexLev doesn't provide a static API key, users on community threads bypass OAuth by grabbing the session identifiers generated by their browser when logged into NexLev, and passing them directly into Antigravity's config file. [[1](https://github.com/czlonkowski/n8n-mcp/blob/main/docs/ANTIGRAVITY_SETUP.md)]\nStep 1: Extract your Session Key from your Browser\nGo to the [NexLev Dashboard](https://dashboard.nexlev.io/) on Google Chrome or Brave and ensure you are logged in.\nPress F12 (or Right-Click -> Inspect) to open Developer Tools.\nHead to the Network tab, click on any recent request on the list (or refresh the page), and look at the Request Headers.\nAlternatively, go to the Application (or Storage) tab -> click Cookies -> click https://nexlev.io.\nFind and copy the value of the cookie handling your session, usually named __session, nexlev_token, or look for the word Bearer eyJ... under the Authorization header.\nStep 2: Manually update mcp_config.json\nAntigravity doesn't have a UI button to install NexLev, so you must manually inject the server configuration. [[1](https://composio.dev/toolkits/reddit/framework/antigravity)]\nOpen Google Antigravity.\nGo to Settings -> Customizations -> Click Open MCP Config.(Alternatively, navigate to C:\\Users\\<YourUsername>\\.gemini\\antigravity\\mcp_config.json on Windows or ~/.gemini/antigravity/mcp_config.json on Mac/Linux).\nAdd the server details using NexLev's official endpoint URL, but append a headers object containing your copied browser credentials so the server immediately recognizes your account without forcing an OAuth browser loop: [[1](https://dashboard.nexlev.io/docs/mcp/quickstart), [2](https://composio.dev/toolkits/reddit/framework/antigravity), [3](https://github.com/czlonkowski/n8n-mcp/blob/main/docs/ANTIGRAVITY_SETUP.md)]\njson\n{ \"mcpServers\": { \"nexlev-mcp\": { \"serverUrl\": \"https://nexlev.io\", \"headers\": { \"Cookie\": \"__session=PASTE_YOUR_COPIED_COOKIE_VALUE_HERE\" } } } }\nUse code with caution.\nNote: If the server expects an authorization token instead of a cookie, replace \"Cookie\": \"...\" with \"Authorization\": \"Bearer PASTE_YOUR_TOKEN_HERE\".\nSave the file and restart Antigravity.\n⚠️ The GitHub Alternative: Proxying through mcp-proxy\nIf injecting headers fails due to Antigravity's remote connection rules, developers on GitHub use a workaround tool called a Local-to-Cloud proxy bridge.\nThey write a simple 3-line local Node.js script that boots up locally on your computer over stdio (which Antigravity loves), handles the authorization locally, and relays the queries to NexLev's servers. [[1](https://github.com/czlonkowski/n8n-mcp/blob/main/docs/ANTIGRAVITY_SETUP.md)]\njson\n{ \"mcpServers\": { \"nexlev-bridge\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@modelcontextprotocol/server-proxy\", \"https://nexlev.io\" ] } } }\nUse code with caution.\nUsing this approach, when you first prompt Antigravity to do YouTube research, the local proxy forces your system terminal to output a physical authorization link. You click it once, log into NexLev via your browser, and the connection remains bridged locally.\nWere you able to find your session token/cookie inside your browser's Dev\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-08-26T00:20:58+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
{"step_index":309,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-25T18:50:59Z","content":"This is exactly what we needed! Two real solutions from actual users. Let me do both right now:\n\n**Approach 1: Cookie injection** — I'll extract the token from your browser\n**Approach 2: mcp-proxy bridge** — I'll set up the npx proxy\n\nLet me check if Antigravity supports headers first, and set up the proxy:","thinking":"The user found the solution via Google AI Mode! There are two approaches that real users use:\n\n1. **Cookie/Token injection**: Extract the session cookie from the browser's DevTools and inject it into the MCP config headers\n2. **mcp-proxy bridge**: Use `npx @modelcontextprotocol/server-proxy` to create a local proxy that handles OAuth\n\nLet me try both approaches. The cookie injection is the fastest - I just need the user to extract their session cookie from the NexLev dashboard.\n\nActually, let me try the mcp-proxy approach first since it's more automated. If the user runs `npx @modelcontextprotocol/server-proxy https://prod.dashboard.nexlev.io/api/claude-mcp`, it should handle the OAuth automatically through the terminal.\nWait, I'm second-guessing whether Antigravity's config schema even supports a headers field for cookie injection — from what I recall of the docs, it only supports command/args for stdio or serverUrl for SSE, not custom headers. I'll still try the cookie approach in case it's supported, but I should focus on walking through the two Google AI Mode approaches, starting with having the user log into the NexLev dashboard and pull the session cookie from DevTools.\n\nAlternatively, I could try the mcp-proxy approach using npx to spin up a local stdio server that proxies to the remote endpoint and handles auth via a terminal link, though I'm not sure the exact package name is right. Given the uncertainty there, I'll just go with the cookie extraction method since it's more straightforward to verify quickly.\n\nI'll try both approaches at once: updating the config to use the npx mcp-remote proxy, and also attempting the cookie injection method. Starting with the npx approach since it seems more promising.","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"C:\\Users\\renu5\\.gemini\\antigravity\\builtin\\skills\\agy-customizations\\docs\\mcp_servers.md","toolAction":"Checking header support","toolSummary":"MCP config schema"}}]}
{"step_index":310,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-25T18:51:27Z","content":"Created At: 2026-08-26T00:21:27+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-08-26T00:21:28+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/renu5/.gemini/antigravity/builtin/skills/agy-customizations/docs/mcp_servers.md`\nTotal Lines: 87\nTotal Bytes: 2966\nShowing lines 1 to 87\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: # MCP Servers (`mcp_config.json`)\n2: \n3: The Model Context Protocol (MCP) is an open standard that enables developers to\n4: build secure, two-way integrations between AI models and their data/tools. In\n5: Antigravity, configuring MCP servers allows you to expose custom tools,\n6: resources, and prompts to the agent.\n7: \n8: MCP servers can be configured globally or packaged within plugins.\n9: \n10: --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n11: \n12: ## Configuration File (`mcp_config.json`)\n13: \n14: MCP servers are defined in a `mcp_config.json` file. The file contains a map of\n15: server identifiers to their respective configurations.\n16: \n17: ### Location\n18: \n19: *   **Global Configuration**: `~/.gemini/config/mcp_config.json` (applies to all\n20:     sessions).\n21: *   **Plugin Configuration**: `plugins/<plugin_name>/mcp_config.json` (active\n22:     when the plugin is enabled).\n23: \n24: --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n25: \n26: ## Configuration Schema\n27: \n28: Antigravity supports two transport mechanisms for MCP: **Stdio** (for local\n29: command-line tools) and **SSE** (for remote services).\n30: \n31: ```json\n32: {\n33:   \"mcpServers\": {\n34:     \"sqlite-helper\": {\n35:       \"command\": \"sqlite-mcp-server\",\n36:       \"args\": [\"/path/to/database.db\"],\n37:       \"env\": {\n38:         \"DB_READONLY\": \"true\"\n39:       }\n40:     },\n41:     \"remote-service\": {\n42:       \"serverUrl\": \"https://mcp.mycompany.com/sse\"\n43:     }\n44:   }\n45: }\n46: ```\n47: \n48: ### 1. Stdio Transport (Local)\n49: \n50: Used to run a local executable or script as an MCP server. The Language Server\n51: spawns the process and communicates with it over standard input/output.\n52: \n53: *   **`command`** (string, required): The executable to run (e.g., `node`,\n54:     `python3`, or a binary name).\n55: *   **`args`** (array of strings, optional): Arguments to pass to the command.\n56: *   **`env`** (object, optional): Environment variables to inject into the\n57:     server process.\n58: \n59: ### 2. SSE Transport (Remote)\n60: \n61: Used to connect to a remote MCP server over HTTP using Server-Sent Events (SSE).\n62: \n63: *   **`serverUrl`** (string, required): The HTTP(S) URL of the remote MCP\n64:     endpoint.\n65: \n66: --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n67: \n68: ## How the Agent Uses MCP\n69: \n70: Once an MCP server is configured and successfully connected:\n71: \n72: 1.  **Tool Discovery**: The system queries the server for its available tools.\n73: 2.  **Tool Injection**: Discovered tools are automatically added to the agent's\n74:     toolset and listed in the system prompt.\n75: 3.  **Execution**: When the agent calls an MCP tool, the Language Server routes\n76:     the request to the MCP server, executes it, and returns the result to the\n77:     agent.\n78: \n79: ## Scoping and Scannability\n80: \n81: *   **Global Servers**: Active for all conversations.\n82: *   **Plugin Servers**: Only active when the parent plugin is loaded. Their\n83:     tools are automatically prefixed or namespaced if necessary to avoid\n84:     conflicts.\n85: *   You can inspect active MCP servers and their tools in the UI by navigating\n86:     to **Additional Options (...) > MCP Servers**.\n87: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n"}
